        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why is my treadmill safety key not working?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually because it is not fully seated, there is dust or lint in the recess holding it away from the switch, its magnet has weakened or come loose, or it is the wrong key for the machine. Reseat it firmly, clean the recess and key, then try a known-good key for the same model. If that also fails, the switch or its wiring is the fault.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use a treadmill without the safety key?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Treadmills are designed not to run without the key, and that is deliberate — it is the machine's emergency stop. Do not bypass it with a magnet, tape or anything else. Order the correct replacement for your model and leave the machine unused until it arrives.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What do I do if I lost my treadmill safety key?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check pockets, the laundry, under and around the machine, and ask whoever might have put it away. If it has gone, find the model and serial number — usually on a sticker near the front of the frame — and order a replacement from the manufacturer's parts or support channel. Order a spare at the same time.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are treadmill safety keys universal?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Keys vary in magnet strength, magnet position and housing shape between brands and sometimes between models. Generic magnetic keys work on some machines, but compare one against the key shown in your manual before buying, and test that pulling it stops the belt promptly. The manufacturer's part for your model is the reliable choice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use a regular magnet as a treadmill safety key?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. A loose magnet taped or stuck over the switch lets the machine run but removes the emergency stop, so the belt keeps moving if you fall, and it lets children start the machine. Use a proper replacement key clipped to your clothing.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my treadmill stop when I move the safety key clip?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The key is barely held in contact — usually a weakened magnet, debris in the recess, or a cord pulling the key at an angle. Clean the recess, let the cord hang freely, and try a known-good key. If the problem persists with a new key, the switch or wiring behind the console may be failing.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

